1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,440
I'm here today with Dr. Tara Thiagarajan, founder of Sapien Labs and the Global Mind Project.

2
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:19,600
You've been doing this for three years now and the instrument that you have used is the MHQ.

3
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:25,380
So tell us about the MHQ, what it is and why you felt the need to create it.

4
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:33,180
Yeah, so the MHQ, which stands for Mental Health Quotient, is really an assessment of overall mental well-being.

5
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:43,660
And I think what's important is to be clear about what we mean by mental well-being because a lot of people conflate mental well-being with mood or happiness.

6
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:45,200
And it's more than that.

7
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:53,460
I like very much the WHO's definition, which aligns with what we're aiming to capture.

8
00:00:53,460 --> 00:01:08,460
And that is that well-being is a state in which you can realize your abilities, navigate, effectively navigate all of the ups and downs and stresses of life and contribute productively and fruitfully to the world.

9
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:25,740
And so what we aim to capture with the MHQ is really an aggregate measure of our full complement of mental capacities and in the context of how those impact our ability to function productively.

10
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:31,680
Yeah, and that's really so amazing that you did it.

11
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:38,980
Because as I understand it, there were many instruments out there that you and the team of scientists at Sapien Labs have studied.

12
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:53,200
And really what there were many ways to be able to diagnose illnesses, but nothing that actually could assess a human being's ability to just go and function and be productive at work or complete an assignment at school.

13
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:54,920
And that was really the motivation.

14
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:55,980
Yeah, absolutely.

15
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:56,580
Absolutely.

16
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:02,700
I think, you know, when we started on this journey, we looked across the whole landscape of assessments in the universe.

17
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,560
You know, there's a lot of psychiatry assessments.

18
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:05,820
There's psychology assessments.

19
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:10,320
There are different kinds of, you know, indices like the happiness index and so on.

20
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:18,580
And psychiatry tends, you know, looks at the human mind from the perspective of things that can go wrong.

21
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:28,140
And so it's a lot about the negative side of function and symptoms and doesn't really capture that full spectrum of positive functioning of well-being as well.

22
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:37,260
And, you know, on the other hand, something like happiness is just one aspect of all of our mental well-being.

23
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:42,660
And, you know, there are measures that really look at just something like life satisfaction or happiness, which are very different.

24
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:45,320
And so there was really a gap.

25
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:51,340
And I think when you want to understand mental well-being and how it's changing, you have to have a way to measure it first.

26
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:53,900
And, you know, that's really step one.

